corpus/bugs.yaml

    Contains 93 short verbatim excerpts, spanning all 61 entries, from public
    forum posts, issue trackers, mailing lists, blog postmortems and vendor
    errata. They sit principally in the
    `evidence:` field, and in a handful of cases in `symptom:` or
    `root_cause:` where the source's own wording is the finding. Each excerpt is a sentence
    or two, is attributed to a specific public URL in the same entry's
    `source:` block, and is included so that a reader can check that the entry
    describes the bug its source describes.

    The count is 83 in `evidence:`, 8 in `symptom:` and 2 in `root_cause:`.
    An earlier revision published 83 -- the `evidence:`-only figure -- while
    the same sentence named the other two fields, so the number understated
    the third-party content by 11%. Reproduce with a count of quoted spans of
    three or more characters over those three fields of corpus/bugs.yaml.

    These excerpts are NOT licensed under Apache-2.0 and are not the project's
    to relicense. They are quoted for identification, criticism and research;
    copyright in each remains with its author. Anyone redistributing this
    corpus should satisfy themselves on that basis independently.

    The corpus records a source URL for every entry. It does not record a
    checksum or a per-source licence determination, which AGENTS.md asks for
    when redistributing vendor artifacts. That gap is real and is tracked
    rather than papered over. An earlier revision said "see the Linear issue
    referenced in corpus/README.md"; that file references no such issue, so
    the claim that the gap was tracked rested on a pointer that does not
    resolve. It is NOT currently tracked in a dedicated issue -- two earlier revisions
    claimed it was, against pointers that did not resolve. The corpus records a
    source URL per entry and no checksum or per-source licence determination,
    and that remains true.

parts/examples/*.part.json

    Electrical values transcribed from manufacturer datasheets, with the
    document, revision and table or section recorded per field in each record's
    `provenance` block. Individual numbers are facts and are not themselves
    copyrightable; the datasheets they were read from are not redistributed and
    are not included here. Each record declares a `license_class` describing
    the terms the underlying document was published under -- a vocabulary
    separate from this repository's own licence, defined in
    parts/part-data.schema.json.

